pinned to its literal `x` position.

The payload shape for the eventBus's `dragEvent`/`resizeEvent` messages —
a `GridItem` reporting its current drag/resize state up to its parent
`GridLayout`. See [GridLayout's eventbus events](/vue/components/grid-layout/eventbus-events/)
for how these messages are actually used.

```ts
interface IEventsData {
  /** The originating event type, e.g. 'dragstart', 'resizemove', 'resizeend'. */
  eventType: string | symbol;
  /** Current height, in grid row units. */
  h: number;
  /** The reporting item's id. */
  i: string | number;
  /** Current width, in grid column units. */
  w: number;
  /** Current horizontal position, in grid column units. */
  x: number;
  /** Current vertical position, in grid row units. */
  y: number;
  /** The originating pointer event's clientX/clientY — real viewport pixel coordinates, not grid units. Drag events only. */
  clientX?: number;
  clientY?: number;
}
```

`clientX`/`clientY` exist specifically so cross-grid drag/drop
(`allowCrossGridDrag`) can check the pointer's position against *other*
grids' bounding rects, using the exact same coordinates the drag itself
is already tracking — see [Drag, drop from grid to grid](/vue/examples/cross-grid-drag-drop/).

```ts
type TLayout<TMeta = unknown> = ILayoutItem<TMeta>[];
```

The full layout for a `GridLayout` — one `ILayoutItem` per rendered
`GridItem`. See [Layout interface](/vue/api/interfaces/layout/) for what
`TMeta` is for.

```ts
type TResponsiveLayout = {
  xxl?: TLayout;
  xl?: TLayout;
  lg?: TLayout;
  md?: TLayout;
  sm?: TLayout;
  xs?: TLayout;
  xxs?: TLayout;
  [key: string]: TLayout | undefined;
};
```

Pre-defined layouts keyed by breakpoint name, for `GridLayout`'s own
`responsiveLayouts` prop. Every key is optional — a breakpoint with no
explicit entry gets an auto-generated layout the first time it's
entered. See [Responsive predefined layouts](/vue/examples/responsive-predefined-layouts/).

:::note[Custom breakpoint names type-check, but aren't resolvable]
The type accepts any string key, but the breakpoint-resolution logic
that actually picks which entry to use can only ever resolve to one of
the 7 standard names (`xxl`/`xl`/`lg`/`md`/`sm`/`xs`/`xxs`) — the same
7 names `breakpoints`/`cols` are fixed to. A `responsiveLayouts` entry
under any other key compiles, but is never actually looked up by the
normal breakpoint-resolution path.
:::

```ts
type TBreakpoint = string;
```

A breakpoint name, e.g. `'lg'`, `'md'`, `'xs'`. Not restricted to the
built-in set at the type level, since `breakpoints`/`cols` can define
custom names (even though, per the note above, only the 7 standard ones
are ever actually resolved to).

```ts
type TBreakpoints = {
  xxl?: number;
  xl?: number;
  lg?: number;
  md?: number;
  sm?: number;
  xs?: number;
  xxs?: number;
};
```

Container-width thresholds per breakpoint name. Structurally identical
to `IBreakpoints` (see [GridLayout props](/vue/components/grid-layout/props/));
both exist in the codebase — prefer `IBreakpoints` for new code
involving `GridLayout` props specifically.

