```ts
interface IGridAriaLabels {
  closeButton?: string;
  itemRoleDescription?: string;
  moveInstruction?: string;
  resizeInstruction?: string;
}
```

| Field | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `closeButton` | `'Close'` | Visually-hidden label for the close button. Read by screen readers; the visible button only shows an icon. |
| `itemRoleDescription` | `'Draggable, resizable item'` | `aria-roledescription` on a draggable/resizable/static-with-neither item's root element. Only applied when the item is actually draggable or resizable — a purely static item has no interactive role to describe. |
| `moveInstruction` | `'Press arrow keys to move.'` | Keyboard instruction text for moving a draggable item, read via `aria-describedby`. Only included when the item is actually draggable. |
| `resizeInstruction` | `'Press shift plus arrow keys to resize.'` | Keyboard instruction text for resizing a resizable item, read via `aria-describedby`. Only included when the item is actually resizable. |

## `DEFAULT_ARIA_LABELS`

```ts
const DEFAULT_ARIA_LABELS: Required<IGridAriaLabels>;
```

The current English text as a real, importable constant — exported so
overriding just one key can spread the rest from this rather than
retyping every default:

```ts
import { DEFAULT_ARIA_LABELS, type IGridAriaLabels } from '@keystone-dashboard-layout/vue';

const gridAriaLabels: IGridAriaLabels = {
  ...DEFAULT_ARIA_LABELS,
  closeButton: 'Supprimer cet élément',
};
```

Three layers merge at runtime, each only overriding the keys it
actually sets: `DEFAULT_ARIA_LABELS` ← `GridLayout`'s own `ariaLabels`
(grid-wide) ← a specific `GridItem`'s own `ariaLabels` (per-item) — see
[Localizable ARIA strings](/vue/examples/aria-labels/) for a full
working example, and [Accessibility scope](/vue/guide/project/accessibility/)
for the wider keyboard/screen-reader picture this fits into.

## `serializeLayout` / `deserializeLayout`

The pure, storage-agnostic building blocks — use these directly for a
backend other than `localStorage`/`sessionStorage` (a remote API, IndexedDB, anything else).

```ts
function serializeLayout(layout: TLayout): string;
function deserializeLayout(json: string | null | undefined): TLayout | null;
```

`serializeLayout` strips the internal `moved` field before
`JSON.stringify`-ing — the one piece of boilerplate every consumer
doing this by hand otherwise has to remember. Never throws.

`deserializeLayout` parses and **validates** the result against the
same shape check `GridLayout` itself runs at mount — a layout that
round-trips through this function is guaranteed to satisfy exactly the
same checks the library enforces elsewhere. Never throws: malformed
JSON, valid JSON that isn't an array of layout items, or an empty/
missing string all return `null` rather than propagating an exception.

## `useLayoutStorage`

```ts
function useLayoutStorage(
  key: string,
  layout: Ref<TLayout>,
  options?: IUseLayoutStorageOptions,
): IUseLayoutStorage;
```

```ts
interface IUseLayoutStorageOptions {
  storage?: Storage;       // default: window.localStorage
  autoLoad?: boolean;      // default: true
  autoSave?: boolean;      // default: false
  debounceMs?: number;     // default: 500 — only relevant when autoSave is true
}

interface IUseLayoutStorage {
  save: () => void;
  load: () => boolean;    // true if a valid layout was found and applied
  clear: () => void;
  hasSaved: () => boolean; // whether *anything* is stored — not whether it's valid
}
```

Wraps `serializeLayout`/`deserializeLayout` for the common
`Storage`-backed case. SSR-safe — every storage access is guarded
behind a `typeof window` check; `autoLoad`/`autoSave` are silent no-ops
during a server render rather than throwing.

## `useLayoutPresets`

```ts
function useLayoutPresets(
  key: string,
  layout: Ref<TLayout>,
  options?: IUseLayoutPresetsOptions,
): IUseLayoutPresets;
```

```ts
interface IUseLayoutPresetsOptions {
  storage?: Storage; // default: window.localStorage
}

interface IUseLayoutPresets {
  savePreset: (name: string) => void;
  loadPreset: (name: string) => boolean;
  deletePreset: (name: string) => void;
  listPresets: () => string[];
  hasPreset: (name: string) => boolean;
}
```

Layers on top of the same `serializeLayout`/`deserializeLayout`
building blocks, for saving and switching between **several named**
arrangements of the same items, rather than one single saved layout.
Every preset for a given `key` is stored together as one object under
one storage key — one read/write per operation, not one per preset.
SSR-safe, same guard as `useLayoutStorage`.

See [v-model & save/load layout](/vue/examples/save-load-layout/) and
[Named layout presets](/vue/examples/named-presets/) for full working
examples of each.

```ts
interface IOutsideItemDropped {
  x: number;
  y: number;
  w: number;
  h: number;
  dataTransfer: DataTransfer | null;
}
```

Payload for `item-dropped-from-outside`. `w`/`h` always echo
`outsideDropWidth`/`outsideDropHeight` back, so a handler doesn't need
to separately track what it configured. `dataTransfer` is the native
object from the browser's own `drop` event — read whatever the dragged
element attached to it (typically via [`readOutsideDropPayload`](/vue/api/interfaces/svg-export-and-payload/))
to decide what this drop actually represents; the library has no way to
know on its own, so nothing is added to `layout` automatically.

```ts
interface ICrossGridItemDropped {
  item: ILayoutItem;
  sourceLayoutId: string;
}
```

Payload for `cross-grid-item-dropped`, fired on the **target** grid —
the moved layout item (already inserted into this grid's `layout`) and
the source grid's `layoutId`.

```ts
interface ICrossGridDropRejected {
  itemId: string | number;
  sourceLayoutId: string;
}
```

Payload for `cross-grid-drop-rejected`, fired on the **target** grid
when it has `disableExternalDrop` set — the item stays in its source
grid; nothing about either grid's `layout` changes.

See [GridLayout events](/vue/components/grid-layout/vue-events/) for
when each of these actually fires, and
[Drag, drop from grid to grid](/vue/examples/cross-grid-drag-drop/) /
[Cross-grid drop restrictions](/vue/examples/cross-grid-drop-restrictions/) /
[Drag, drop from outside](/vue/examples/outside-drag-drop/) for working
examples.

These type the values a `ref<InstanceType<typeof GridLayout>>()` (or
`GridItem`) can read directly, or the scoped props of the
[`#placeholder`](/vue/components/grid-layout/slots/) slot — most
consumers never need to import these explicitly, since accessing them
through a template ref is already correctly typed without it.

```ts
interface IPlaceholder {
  h: number;
  i: number | string;
  x: number;
  y: number;
  w: number;
}
```

Grid-unit position/size of `GridLayout`'s internal drag placeholder —
the hidden `GridItem` shown while a drag or resize is in progress.
Reachable as `gridRef.value.placeholder`, and as the `placeholder`
scoped prop on the `#placeholder` slot.

```ts
interface IAlignmentGuide {
  axis: 'x' | 'y';
  position: number;
}
```

One entry in `gridRef.value.alignmentGuides` — populated during a
drag/resize when `showAlignmentGuides` is on. `axis: 'x'` is a vertical
guide line (a left/right edge alignment) at grid-column `position`;
`axis: 'y'` is horizontal (a top/bottom alignment) at grid-row
`position`.

```ts
interface ISpacingIndicator {
  axis: 'x' | 'y';
  gapStart: number;
  gapEnd: number;
  distance: number;
}
```

One entry in `gridRef.value.spacingIndicators` — the nearest-neighbor
gap on one side of the active item, labeled with `distance`
(`gapEnd - gapStart`), when `showSpacingGuides` is on.

```ts
interface IGridItemPosition {
  left?: number;
  right?: number;
  top: number;
  width: number;
  height: number;
}
```

Live pixel position/size for a `GridItem` during an active drag
(`left`/`top`, or `right`/`top` when rendering RTL) or resize
(`width`/`height`).

See [Alignment guides while dragging](/vue/examples/alignment-guides/)
for a working example using `showAlignmentGuides`.
